# Standard perft positions - the six everyone benchmarks against.
#
# Format:  <fen> ;D<depth> <expected leaf nodes> ;D<depth> <count> ...
# Run:     ./chessengine perft suite tests/perft/standard.epd [maxdepth]
#
# The counts below are the published, universally agreed values. If your
# movegen disagrees with ANY of them, it is wrong - do not rationalise it.
# Use `./chessengine perft <depth>` after `position fen ...` to get a
# per-move breakdown, compare it against Stockfish's `go perft <depth>`,
# and recurse into the first move whose count differs.
#
# Deep entries are slow (position 2 at D5 is ~194M nodes), so CI passes a
# maxdepth of 4. Run the full suite by hand before any release.

# 1. Starting position.
rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1 ;D1 20 ;D2 400 ;D3 8902 ;D4 197281 ;D5 4865609 ;D6 119060324

# 2. "Kiwipete" - dense middlegame exercising castling, pins and captures.
#    The single most effective position for shaking out movegen bugs.
r3k2r/p1ppqpb1/bn2pnp1/3PN3/1p2P3/2N2Q1p/PPPBBPPP/R3K2R w KQkq - 0 1 ;D1 48 ;D2 2039 ;D3 97862 ;D4 4085603 ;D5 193690690

# 3. Sparse endgame - heavy on promotions and en passant.
8/2p5/3p4/KP5r/1R3p1k/8/4P1P1/8 w - - 0 1 ;D1 14 ;D2 191 ;D3 2812 ;D4 43238 ;D5 674624 ;D6 11030083

# 4. Promotion-rich position with castling rights on one side.
r3k2r/Pppp1ppp/1b3nbN/nP6/BBP1P3/q4N2/Pp1P2PP/R2Q1RK1 w kq - 0 1 ;D1 6 ;D2 264 ;D3 9467 ;D4 422333 ;D5 15833292

# 5. Mirror of position 4, to catch colour-asymmetric bugs.
r2q1rk1/pP1p2pp/Q4n2/bbp1p3/Np6/1B3NBn/pPPP1PPP/R3K2R b KQ - 0 1 ;D1 6 ;D2 264 ;D3 9467 ;D4 422333 ;D5 15833292

# 6. Position 5 from the CPW list - no castling, tactical.
rnbq1k1r/pp1Pbppp/2p5/8/2B5/8/PPP1NnPP/RNBQK2R w KQ - 1 8 ;D1 44 ;D2 1486 ;D3 62379 ;D4 2103487 ;D5 89941194

# 7. Position 6 from the CPW list - quiet, balanced, many legal moves.
r4rk1/1pp1qppp/p1np1n2/2b1p1B1/2B1P1b1/P1NP1N2/1PP1QPPP/R4RK1 w - - 0 10 ;D1 46 ;D2 2079 ;D3 89890 ;D4 3894594 ;D5 164075551
